---

<Base
  title="Research Program · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="How the work proceeds in general: begin with a question about linguistic meaning, work out what each account predicts for later reasoning, and only then choose a method that can separate those predictions."
>
  <article class="rp">
    <h1 class="rp-h1">Research Program</h1>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Language first</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Language comes first. Each project starts from a disagreement about meaning, works out what
        each account predicts for the reasoning that follows, and only then chooses a method that can
        separate those predictions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">From analysis to predictions</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Formal semantics offers explicit alternatives rather than a general claim that a system
        “understands” an expression. For each account, the questions are what should remain possible
        after an utterance, which inferences it should license, and what later evidence should change.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Those predictions determine the experiment. Depending on the question, the relevant evidence
        may come from behavioural work, controlled model training, diagnostic evaluation, or
        intervention. The method serves the linguistic contrast; it does not define the research
        identity.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Two empirical questions</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The same linguistic analysis leads to two questions. The developmental question asks how the
        function becomes available in human cognition. The computational question asks whether
        linguistic input is sufficient for a learner to acquire it, and which features of that input
        matter.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        The questions meet at the linguistic account, but they are not versions of the same
        experiment. Evidence about human development does not tell us what a model learned, and
        evidence about a model does not explain how a child acquires language.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Why language models</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        A language model offers two useful forms of control. Its training input can be specified, and
        its internal states can be measured or intervened on. This makes it possible to ask what a
        linguistic distribution can teach and which learned representations are causally involved in a
        behaviour. Internal structure matters only when it explains a well-defined behavioural effect.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Beyond modality</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Epistemic modality is the first case because it provides explicit competing analyses and clear
        questions about later reasoning. The same approach could extend to evidentials, conditionals,
        negation, counterfactuals, and propositional attitudes. In each case, the task is to state the
        linguistic function precisely enough that evidence can count for or against it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="rp-foot"><a href="/research">See how this program is being developed in the current work →</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

